i think a STRING LITERAL might be better in most cases. for instance:

```
rule:
     echo ${"表":Q}
```

for this string literal we expect "\a\b\t\n\v\f\r\e\s\x{...}" escapes (\s is space; from java 15); namely we require braces, since we do want to emphasize that this is a code point.

this expansion may or may not work in prerequisites; i don't mind either way.... but it is a bit redundant considering that we have ${U+hex} escapes there, so it'd be silly.

you can extend gengenlib for this (no need to bump version for that either); this is general and narrow enough, but don't imply `:Q`, since this should be valid outside of parameter expansion.

we can still have variables though.
the specs are as follows:

they are basically as follows:

```
var=h u h  # this is a comment
      a${"\s"}
       b
      # not a comment${"\s"}
# comment
      c
      ${""}
```

this gives:

```
var="h u h\na \n b\n# not a comment \nc\n"
```

the trailing newline is preserved by the empty string on the last line (it could have also been a lone tab, but it may not look as obvious so i did this.) (also, tab indentation is required)

one thing is that for readability:

```
var=          # this is a comment
      h u h
      a\s
       b
      # not a comment\s
# comment
      c
      ${""}
```

though the line at "h u h" would actually be interpreted literally even if there is "#" in the middle.

variable expansion should also work, but immediately evaluated (no recursive references!) and excluding the :Q parameter expansion.

also: note that `${"\n":Q}` is not really escapable (not to the shell, but to Make: so have the backend check if this would be misinterpreted as a new command!) but `${"\r":Q}` is (e.g. `${"Icon\r":Q}` in the infamous macOS's case.)

